<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Vajji</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 468 BCE (approx.) · capital Vaishali</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of republican clans around Vaishali, praised by the Buddha for its assemblies and remembered as one of the world's early republics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Vajji league formed north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, uniting eight or nine clans. The most powerful were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a>; others included the Videhas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jnatrikas">Jnatrikas</a>, the clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> teacher, who was born near Vaishali. By the 6th century BCE it was one of the sixteen mahajanapadas.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Vajjis were governed by assemblies of the heads of noble families, who met in council and elected leaders. In the Mahaparinibbana Sutta the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a> says the Vajjis will prosper as long as they meet often, act in concord and honour their traditions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> was a large trading city with many shrines and was visited often by both the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a> built a fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataligrama</a>, the future Pataliputra, and sent his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassakara">Vassakara</a> to sow discord among the Vajjis before conquering them after a long war in the 5th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> later hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-buddhist-council">Second Buddhist Council</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vajji.jpg" alt="Vajji" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: Antiquistik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gana_Sanghas_(c._500_BCE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 468 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vaishali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jainism, Buddhism, Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vajjika League, Vrijji, Licchavi republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-584&amp;focus=vajji" aria-label="Vajji on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6930 1223 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vajji lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7250 1479L7325 1576M7250 1479L7266 1513"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7325 1576h0M7266 1513h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7250 1479h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7250 1479h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vaishali. <a href="/atlas?year=-584&amp;focus=vajji">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
